&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem With Skipping the Setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Solana has no account recovery system. No support ticket, no "forgot password" flow, no custodian holding a backup. When you create a self-custody wallet, you become the sole keyholder. Skip one step during setup and the consequences are permanent. This post covers the setup correctly, with the on-chain mechanics that matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Pick a Wallet App
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The three wallet apps most Solana devs and traders actually use:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Phantom:&lt;/strong&gt; browser extension and mobile app, broad dApp support across the Solana ecosystem.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Solflare:&lt;/strong&gt; extension and mobile app, built for Solana from day one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backpack:&lt;/strong&gt; extension and mobile app, includes an optional built-in exchange account.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All three support SOL and SPL tokens, transaction signing, and wallet connections via the Solana wallet adapter. Installation source matters: get the extension from the official site or official app store listing only. Cloned extensions with matching names and icons do exist, and their only job is to steal your recovery phrase the moment you type it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For larger holdings, pairing with a hardware signer (Ledger, for example) moves transaction approval off the browser and onto a physical device. That adds a meaningful layer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How the Recovery Phrase Works On-Chain
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a wallet is created, it derives a keypair from a 12 or 24 word BIP-39 mnemonic. The public key becomes your on-chain address. The private key signs transactions. The mnemonic is the root of all of that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What that means practically:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whoever has the mnemonic controls the wallet from any device, anywhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;There is no on-chain revocation mechanism. A stolen phrase means a lost wallet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write the words on paper, in sequence, and store that paper somewhere physically secure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Don't photograph it, paste it into Notes, email it, or sync it to any cloud service.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No wallet team, support channel, or swap app will ever need your mnemonic. Any request for it is a scam.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The app password you set during setup is a local encryption key for the device. It doesn't back anything up and can't restore the wallet on another machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  SOL, Fees, and Token Account Rent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every Solana transaction burns a small amount of SOL as a network fee. Beyond that, Solana's account model requires a funded on-chain account to store each SPL token balance. Opening a new token account locks roughly 0.002 SOL as &lt;a href="https://docs.solanalabs.com/implemented-proposals/rent" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;rent&lt;/a&gt;, held as a deposit for as long as that account exists. Swapping into a token you've never held before triggers this cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To fund the wallet:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Copy your public address from the wallet UI. Sharing this is fine.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Withdraw SOL from a CEX to that address, selecting the Solana network at withdrawal.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Send a small test amount first, confirm it arrives, then send the rest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running out of SOL mid-session is one of the more common failure modes. Keep a buffer. For typical swap activity, a few hundredths of a SOL is enough for fees and account rent across several transactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Connecting to a Swap App
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Swap apps use the Solana wallet adapter. Clicking "Connect Wallet" triggers a handshake: the app reads your public key and that's the extent of it. The connection grants zero signing authority. Your private key never leaves the wallet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That said, verify the domain in your browser bar before connecting. Phishing sites reproduce swap UIs closely. Bookmark the apps you actually use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://swap.io/s/SOL-USDC?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=how-to-set-up-a-solana-wallet-for-your-first-swap" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;swap.io&lt;/a&gt;, a Solana swap aggregator, is one option. It compares quotes from multiple routing providers, Jupiter among them, and shows the full route and minimum received before you sign anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to Check Before Signing a Transaction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The wallet surfaces a transaction payload before you approve it. Don't skip this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Token addresses, not just names or logos.&lt;/strong&gt; Anyone can deploy a token called "USDC" with a matching icon.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Input and output amounts&lt;/strong&gt; match what the swap UI showed you.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Minimum received&lt;/strong&gt; is the floor you've agreed to. Below that, the transaction reverts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the wallet flags a transaction as unusual, or you can't map what's on screen to what you intended, cancel and investigate. The &lt;a href="https://swap.io/learn/how-to-swap-tokens-on-solana/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=how-to-set-up-a-solana-wallet-for-your-first-swap" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;step-by-step guide to swapping tokens on Solana&lt;/a&gt; walks through each field on the swap screen if you want a visual reference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Ongoing Wallet Hygiene
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unknown tokens appearing in your wallet&lt;/strong&gt; are almost always spam, tokens airdropped to bait you into visiting a malicious site. Don't interact with them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Connected app permissions&lt;/strong&gt; accumulate over time. Review them in your wallet's settings and revoke anything you no longer use.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A separate hot wallet&lt;/strong&gt; for experimenting with new protocols limits blast radius. Load only what you need for the session.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Key Takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Only install wallet software from official sources; clones exist and are designed to steal keys.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The recovery phrase derives your keypair. It's the wallet. Store it offline and share it with nobody.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SOL is required for fees and for SPL token account rent, even when swapping stablecoins.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Connecting a wallet only exposes your public key. Always verify the domain before connecting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Read the token address and minimum received in the transaction payload before approving.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Do I need a centralized exchange account to swap on Solana?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. On-chain swap apps connect directly to a self-custody wallet. You do need SOL in that wallet to cover network fees and any account rent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Can I use one recovery phrase across both mobile and desktop?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Importing the same mnemonic into the official mobile app and the official extension gives you access from both. Only do this inside the official app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What happens if I lose my recovery phrase?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you still have device access, create a new wallet, back up its phrase correctly, and transfer your assets across immediately. If you've lost both the device and the phrase, the wallet is gone with no recourse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How much SOL should I keep as a buffer?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fees per transaction are small, but token account rent at roughly 0.002 SOL per new account and any priority fees add up across multiple swaps. A buffer of a few hundredths of a SOL handles most typical activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to try the full flow, the &lt;a href="https://swap.io/dust-sweeper?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=how-to-set-up-a-solana-wallet-for-your-first-swap" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;swap.io Dust Sweeper&lt;/a&gt; is a practical first step: it closes empty token accounts, clears spam tokens, and returns the locked SOL rent to your wallet before you get into active trading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hi everyone, I'm Antonio. I'm 53, I work as a factory worker during the day, and in the evening I keep building my own projects: modules, classes, a small 2D game engine and a debugging tool called &lt;strong&gt;BeeLadybug&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Last time I told you how I &lt;a href="https://dev.to/antonioprosperi2svg/i-wanted-a-floating-search-bar-the-fix-was-a-few-lines-of-css-after-hours-of-arguing-with-ai-272f"&gt;argued with AI for hours over a floating search bar&lt;/a&gt; that only needed a few lines of CSS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This time the story is different. This time the AIs were more bugged than my code. 😂&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What BeeLadybug is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BeeLadybug is an open-source inspection overlay that runs inside a Shadow DOM. You send it raw data, and adapters turn the page into something you can read:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bee-ladybug/src/index.js'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BeeLadybugCore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WebDOMAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CSSAdapter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bee&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BeeLadybugCore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WebDOMAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bee&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;attach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CSSAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bee&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;attach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bee&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bee&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Paste that in the console of any page (Chrome may ask you to type &lt;code&gt;allow pasting&lt;/code&gt; first), press &lt;strong&gt;F6&lt;/strong&gt; to start the CSS hunt, and &lt;strong&gt;F8&lt;/strong&gt; to export a full HTML report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The plan: just write the docs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm writing the documentation one adapter at a time. Canvas, WebDOM, and then &lt;strong&gt;CSSAdapter&lt;/strong&gt;, about 1,235 lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To document it properly I asked an AI to read the module and explain it to me. Instead of an explanation, I got a list of bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I asked a second AI to check the same folder. It found a &lt;em&gt;different&lt;/em&gt; list. One said "10 functions" and then listed 11. Another said "5 points" and wrote 6.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a full day of work, reading 1,500 lines of code with two AIs arguing in my head... my brain got bugged too. 😂&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How I got out of it: I checked with my own eyes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I stopped trusting the numbers and opened the file myself. One of the claims was that the &lt;code&gt;S&lt;/code&gt; key scan didn't filter the same noise as the F8 report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Line 144, inside &lt;code&gt;scan()&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isBeeChrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isSkippedTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Line 216, inside &lt;code&gt;snapshot()&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isBeeChrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isSkippedTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isReportNoise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There it was. The quick scan was missing &lt;code&gt;isReportNoise(el)&lt;/code&gt;, so on a WordPress site it reported fake problems on &lt;code&gt;#wpadminbar&lt;/code&gt; and on hidden elements, while the F8 report skipped them correctly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bug was real. The counting was wrong, but the bugs were real.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The 11 bugs (short version)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Merging both reviews, these were the ones worth fixing now:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;With the CSS hunt on, &lt;strong&gt;Ctrl+S / Ctrl+P / Cmd+S were blocked&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;code&gt;#handleKey&lt;/code&gt; didn't check modifier keys.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;With the overlay hidden (F2), the &lt;code&gt;P&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;S&lt;/code&gt; keys and the hover/pin boxes &lt;strong&gt;were still active&lt;/strong&gt; on the page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A stylesheet change before the first mouse move inspected the element at &lt;strong&gt;(0,0)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;scan()&lt;/code&gt; didn't filter &lt;code&gt;isReportNoise&lt;/code&gt;, as shown above.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Modern colors like &lt;code&gt;oklch()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;lab()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;color()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;color-mix()&lt;/code&gt; produced a &lt;strong&gt;fake "invisible text"&lt;/strong&gt; warning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Stylesheets that were &lt;strong&gt;disabled&lt;/strong&gt; or had a non-matching &lt;code&gt;media&lt;/code&gt; (like &lt;code&gt;print&lt;/code&gt;) were counted as active.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The snapshot cut to 80 rows &lt;strong&gt;before&lt;/strong&gt; sorting by severity, so a harmless &lt;code&gt;will-change&lt;/code&gt; could push out a real error.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;touchesSheet&lt;/code&gt; checked the whole subtree on every mutation, which invalidated the cache almost every frame.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;HUD states (&lt;code&gt;css.target&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;css.hits&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;css.pin&lt;/code&gt;) &lt;strong&gt;stayed stuck&lt;/strong&gt; after turning the hunt off.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;cssPath&lt;/code&gt; didn't escape special characters, which breaks with Tailwind classes like &lt;code&gt;sm:w-1/2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The other adapters didn't recognize CSSAdapter's own boxes (&lt;code&gt;data-bee-ladybug-css&lt;/code&gt;), so they could count them as page nodes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One small fix, as an example. The keyboard shortcut problem was solved like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;#&lt;span class="nf"&gt;handleKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctrlKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;metaKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;altKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;core&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;core&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ... S scans, P pins&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  The result
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;npm test&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;strong&gt;70 tests, 70 passing&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;npm run lint&lt;/code&gt;: clean&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;npm run typecheck&lt;/code&gt;: clean&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The things that are design limits, not bugs (like overflow inside &lt;code&gt;overflow:hidden&lt;/code&gt; ancestors, or &lt;code&gt;@import&lt;/code&gt; sheets not being visible), will go in the docs as &lt;strong&gt;known limitations&lt;/strong&gt;. I think that's more honest than pretending they don't exist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I learned
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Writing docs is a code review.&lt;/strong&gt; When you have to explain a function, you notice when it doesn't do what you think.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Two AIs disagreeing doesn't mean nobody is right.&lt;/strong&gt; It means you have to open the file and look.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Don't trust counts, trust lines.&lt;/strong&gt; "Line 144 vs line 216" is a fact; "10 or 11 bugs" is just noise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You don't need a degree to read code. You need patience, coffee, and the stubbornness to check one line at a time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BeeLadybug is on npm as &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/bee-ladybug" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;bee-ladybug&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, and the docs (in Italian, for now) are on &lt;a href="https://beeenginejs.com/ladybug/inizia/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;beeenginejs.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're also building things at night after work: keep going. Your brain will get bugged, but your code will get better. 🐞&lt;/p&gt;

